{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"14ef439c-ceea-40c8-b2ba-cd9e0249839b","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A positive point charge creates an electric field directed radially outward. The arrows show the force direction on a positive test charge.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Electric field of a point charge"},{"op":"diagram","id":"field","region":"right","primitives":[{"kind":"point","at":[0.4,0.5],"label":"+Q","meaning":"Positive source charge"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.4,0.42],[0.4,0.2]],"stroke":"solid","arrow":true},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.4,0.58],[0.4,0.8]],"stroke":"solid","arrow":true},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.32,0.5],[0.1,0.5]],"stroke":"solid","arrow":true},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.34,0.42],[0.19,0.22]],"stroke":"solid","arrow":true},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.46,0.58],[0.61,0.78]],"stroke":"solid","arrow":true}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"14ef439c-ceea-40c8-b2ba-cd9e0249839b","step":{"id":"s2","script":"At distance r, field strength is k times the source charge magnitude divided by r squared. Field means force per unit positive test charge.","ops":[{"op":"point","id":"near","canvas_id":"field","at":[0.62,0.5],"label":"r"},{"op":"arrow","id":"nearfield","canvas_id":"field","from":[0.62,0.5],"to":[0.76,0.5],"stroke":"solid","label":"E"},{"op":"equation","id":"fieldlaw","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"E=\\frac{k|Q|}{r^2},\\qquad k=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}"},{"op":"equation","id":"definition","anchor":{"el":"fieldlaw","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\vec E=\\frac{\\vec F}{q_{\\mathrm{test}}}\\quad(q_{\\mathrm{test}}>0)"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"14ef439c-ceea-40c8-b2ba-cd9e0249839b","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Doubling the distance reduces field strength to one quarter. For a negative source charge, all field arrows reverse and point inward.","ops":[{"op":"point","id":"far","canvas_id":"field","at":[0.84,0.5],"label":"2r"},{"op":"arrow","id":"farfield","canvas_id":"field","from":[0.84,0.5],"to":[0.875,0.5],"stroke":"solid","label":"E/4"},{"op":"equation","id":"scaling","anchor":{"el":"definition","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"E(2r)=\\frac{k|Q|}{(2r)^2}=\\frac{E(r)}{4}"},{"op":"text","id":"direction","anchor":{"el":"scaling","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"Positive: outward. Negative: inward."}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If the distance triples, what fraction of the original field strength remains?","expected_gist":"One ninth, because field strength varies inversely with distance squared."}}}
